 imposes a canonical solution path (i.e., a convergent set of tool invocations shared across successful runs) and agent success depends critically on whether a trajectory stays within this path's operating envelope. We establish this causally using a natural experiment that holds model capability and task difficulty fixed by construction. \nRobustness and generalization of the canonical path adherence effect. (A) Within-unit adherence gaps across 70 tasks; 56/70 (80%) show positive effects (mean = +0.068, green dashed line). (B) Effect size is larger for shorter tasks (í µí± = −0.25, í µí± = 0.038), consistent with less opportunity for course-correction after deviation; colour indicates canonical path strength. (C) All 11 model families show positive gaps; 5 reach individual significance (í µí± < 0.05, dark blue). Error bars show ±1.96 SE.… \n\nDataset structure and mixed-outcome sample.… \n\nFigures - available via license: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\n\nCapable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal\n\nMechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks\n\nWilson Y. Lee\n\nwilson.yenhsun.lee@gmail.com\n\nIndependent Researcher\n\nUnited States\n\nAbstract\n\nWhy do language agents fail on tasks they are capable of solving?\n.\n\n0001,\n\n𝑛=\n\n488 units, 95%\n\nCI [+0.043, +0.077]). This result survives six robustness checks\n\nincluding cross-model-family leave-one-out validation, which rules\n\nout both circularity and family-level confounding. Critically, the\n\ncausal mechanism is gradual and self-reinforcing: the adherence gap\n\nis statistically indistinguishable from zero through the rst 50% of\n\nthe trajectory, ruling out early-branching selection bias, and each\n\no-canonical tool call raises the probability that the next call is also\n\no-canonical by 22.7 percentage points (\n\nˆ\n\n𝛽=+\n\n0\n\n.\n\n227,\n\n𝑝<\n\n0\n\n.\n\n0001),\n\nmore than doubling the baseline o-canonical rate. These ndings\n\nimply that agent reliability cannot be improved by capability scaling\n\nalone, but oer a highly actionable intervention: a simple monitor\n\nthat restarts the bottom tercile of runs based on mid-trajectory\n\ncanonical adherence lifts success rates by\n\n+\n\n8.8 percentage points\n\namong intervened runs.\n\nKeywords\n\nLLM agents, tool-use, agent reliability, causal inference, benchmark-\n\ning, stochastic drift\n\n1 Introduction\n\nWhy do language agents fail? The dominant answer in the lit-\n\nerature is capability: agents fail because they lack the knowledge,\n\nreasoning ability, or tool-use skill to solve the task [\n\n12\n\n,\n\n15\n\n,\n\n20\n\n].\n\nThis framing has driven the eld’s response—better models, longer\n\ncontext windows, improved reasoning chains, more sophisticated\n\nprompting. Yet a puzzling empirical regularity challenges this ac-\n\ncount. When the same frontier model attempts the same task mul-\n\ntiple times under identical conditions, it sometimes succeeds and\n\nsometimes fails. A model that cannot solve a task should fail consis-\n\ntently; a model that can should succeed consistently. The existence\n\nof mixed outcomes—the same capable model succeeding on one\n\nrun and failing on another—suggests that something other than\n\ncapability is determining success on a substantial fraction of agent\n\nfailures.\n\nWe propose that many agent failures are reliability failures, not\n\ncapability failures. LLM agents are stochastic systems: temperature-\n\nbased sampling introduces randomness at every decision point,\n\ncausing the same model to produce dierent tool sequences across\n\nruns. When this stochasticity pushes a trajectory away from the\n\ntask’s solution structure, failure results—not because the model\n\nlacks capability, but because this particular draw from its distribu-\n\ntion happened to diverge from the region of behavior that leads\n\nto success. This distinction matters: capability failures call for bet-\n\nter models, but reliability failures call for better constraints on\n\nstochastic behavior during execution.\n\nThe central concept we introduce is the canonical solution\n\npath. For any well-dened task solvable by tool-using agents, suc-\n\ncessful runs across dierent models tend to converge on similar\n\ntool-use strategies—a set of tools whose invocation is characteristic\n\nof task completion. We operationalize this convergent structure as\n\nthe empirical consensus tool set: tools used in more than 50% of suc-\n\ncessful runs across models. This set is the operationalization of the\n\ncanonical solution path—the formal construct we use to measure\n\nhow closely a trajectory adheres to the task’s dominant success-\n\nful strategy. This convergence is a property of the task, not the\n\nmodel. It emerges from the information dependencies imposed by\n\nthe task environment: les must be read before they can be edited;\n\nsearch results must be retrieved before they can be synthesized;\n\ndata must be validated before it can be submitted. These natural\n\norderings of information dependencies create a limited set of viable\n\ntool sequences—the canonical path. We show empirically that 58%\n\nof tasks in a large-scale agent benchmark exhibit strong canonical\n\npaths (mean Jaccard similarity\n\n>\n\n0.6 among successful runs across\n\ndierent models).\n\nWe provide within-unit causal evidence that canonical path ad-\n\nherence causally predicts success within mixed-outcome units, un-\n\nder a standard exogeneity assumption. Our identication strategy\n\nexploits a natural experiment (an unplanned source of variation\n\nthat approximates random assignment) embedded in the Toolathlon\n\nbenchmark [\n\n5\n\n], which contains 22 frontier models each attempting\n\n108 real-world tool-use tasks across 3 independent runs. Among\n\nthe 515 model\n\n×\n\ntask units where outcomes vary across runs—pairs\n\nwhere the same model succeeds on some runs and fails on others—\n\nvariation in outcomes cannot be attributed to model capability\n\nor task diculty, both of which are held xed within units. The\n\narXiv:2602.19008v1 [cs.CL] 22 Feb 2026\n\nLee\n\n10% 25% 50% 75% 100%\n\nreport variance across runs alongside mean performance. For our\n\ntheoretical understanding of agent failure, the gradual drift mech-\n\nanism suggests that mid-trajectory monitoring and correction—\n\ndetecting canonical path deviation before it compounds—may be\n\nmore eective than initialization-time interventions such as better\n\nprompting or chain-of-thought.\n\n2 Related Work\n\nLLM agent benchmarks. A growing body of work evaluates LLM\n\nagents on real-world tool-use tasks [\n\n7\n\n,\n\n17\n\n,\n\n18\n\n,\n\n22\n\n]. These bench-\n\nmarks primarily measure capability—typically reported as pass@1\n\nor pass@\n\n𝑘\n\n—and treat failures as evidence of insucient model abil-\n\nity. A notable exception is\n\n𝜏\n\n-bench [\n\n19\n\n], which introduces the Pˆ\n\n𝑘\n\nCapable but Unreliable: Agent Failure via Canonical Path Dri\n\nmetric (the probability of succeeding on all\n\n𝑘\n\nindependent trials) to\n\nsurface the reliability gap between what models can occasionally\n\nachieve and what they achieve consistently.\n\n𝜏\n\n-bench establishes\n0\n\n.\n\n01,\n\n𝑝=\n\n0\n\n.\n\n92), conrming that canonical paths reect task\n\nstructure rather than task diculty. Canonical paths are predomi-\n\nnantly generic: only 13.5% of canonical tools on average contain\n\ntask-domain identiers, indicating that canonical paths capture\n\ngeneral solution strategies, not trivially obvious tool choices.\n\n4.3 Cross-Family Validation\n\nTo ensure that canonical paths are genuine task-level properties\n\nand not artifacts of the behavior of any particular model family\n\nCapable but Unreliable: Agent Failure via Canonical Path Dri\n\n(i.e., same organization or base model lineage), we employ a cross-\n\nfamily leave-one-out (CF-LOO) denition. For a trajectory from\n\nmodel family\n\n𝑓\n\non task\n\n𝑗\n\n, the CF-LOO canonical set excludes all\n\nsuccessful runs from family 𝑓:\n\n𝐶−𝑓\n\n𝑗=\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n𝑡Í 𝜏∈ S −𝑓\n\n𝑗\n\n1[𝑡∈ T (𝜏)]\n\n|S −𝑓\n\n𝑗|\n\n>0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n(3)\n\nwhere\n\nS−𝑓\n\n𝑗\n\nexcludes all successful runs from family\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCapable but Unreliable | OpenTrain AI (https://www.opentrain.ai/papers/capable-but-unreliable-canonical-path-deviation-as-a-causal-mechanism-of-agent-f--arxiv-2602.19008/)\nciteturn32032search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: today; Capable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks ... Lee Published Feb 22, 2026 ... These findings imply that agent reliability cannot be improved by capability scaling alone, but offer a highly actionable intervention: a simple monitor that restarts the bottom tercile of runs based on mid-trajectory canonical adherence lifts success rates by $+$8.8 percentage points among intervened runs.\n  4.   5. Capable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks\n\n# Capable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks\n\nWilson Y. Lee Published Feb 22, 2026\n\narXiv PDF [Button: Copy citation] [Button: Copy BibTeX]\n\nResearcher verdict\n\nContext only\n\nUse as context only\n\nBenchmark evidence\n\nThin evidence\n\nVerify before relying\n\nTime to first repro\n\nA few days\n\nPlan setup time\n\nRisk flags\n\n2\n\nReview before use\n\n## Abstract\n\nDomain fit: AI-adjacent · Paper appears method- or tooling-adjacent to AI workflows with partial ecosystem coverage.\n\nWhy do language agents fail on tasks they are capable of solving? We argue that many such failures are reliability failures caused by stochastic drift from a task's latent solution structure, not capability failures. Every well-defined tool-use task imposes a canonical solution path (i.e., a convergent set of tool invocations shared across successful runs) and agent success depends critically on whether a trajectory stays within this path's operating envelope. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCapable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks - Science Explorer Abstract (https://scixplorer.org/abs/2026arXiv260219008L/abstract)\nciteturn32032search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; ## Capable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks ... Publication Date  | February 2026\n\nAbstract\n\nAbstract\n\nCitations 3\n\nReferences 3\n\nCredits\n\nMentions\n\nCo-Reads\n\nSimilar Papers\n\nVolume Content\n\nGraphics\n\nMetrics\n\nExport Citation\n\n## Capable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks\n\n### Authors\n\nLee, Wilson Y.\n\nEprint\n\nFull Text Sources\n\nOther Resources\n\n### Abstract\n\nWhy do language agents fail on tasks they are capable of solving? We argue that many such failures are reliability failures caused by stochastic drift from a task's latent solution structure, not capability failures. Every well-defined tool-use task imposes a canonical solution path (i.e., a convergent set of tool invocations shared across successful runs) and agent success depends critically on whether a trajectory stays within this path's operating envelope. We establish this causally using a natural experiment that holds model capability and task difficulty fixed by construction. We analyze trajectories from the Toolathlon benchmark: 22 frontier models each attempt 108 real-world tool-use tasks across 3 independent runs, yielding 515 model$\\times$task units where the same model succeeds on some runs and fails on others due to LLM sampling stochasticity alone. Within these units, successful runs adhere significantly more closely to the canonical solution path than failed runs ($+$0.060 Jaccard, $p<0.0001$, $n=488$ units, 95% CI [+0.043, +0.077]). This result survives six robustness checks including cross-model-family leave-one-out validation. Critically, the causal mechanism is gradual and self-reinforcing: the adherence gap is statistically indistinguishable from zero through the first 50% of the trajectory, ruling out early-branching selection bias, and each off-canonical tool call raises the probability that the next call is also off-canonical by 22.7 percentage points ($\\hatβ=+0.227$, $p<0.0001$), more than doubling the baseline rate. These findings imply that agent reliability cannot be improved by capability scaling alone, but offer a highly actionable intervention: a simple monitor that restarts the bottom tercile of runs based on mid-trajectory canonical adherence lifts success rates by $+$8.8 percentage points among intervened runs.\n\n### Details\n\nPublication  | eprint arXiv:2602.19008\nPublication Date  | February 2026\nDOI  | 10.48550/arXiv.2602.19008\narXiv  | arXiv:2602.19008\nBibcode  | 2026arXiv260219008L\nCollection  | general\nKeywords  | Computation and Language Machine Learning\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCapable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks | Article Information | J-GLOBAL (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/en/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202602208713606417)\nciteturn32032search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Capable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks ... Publication year： Feb. 22, 2026  update date on preprint server： Feb.\n\nPreprint\n\nJ-GLOBAL ID：202602208713606417   Reference number：26P0052452\n\n# Capable but Unreliable: Canonic